    title: "SDA Theological Integration",
    content: "The Adventist doctrinal formulation of baptism addresses three major questions: the mode, the subjects, and the significance.\n\n**Mode: immersion.** Adventists practice baptism by full immersion for several converging reasons. The Greek word baptizo means to plunge, dip, or submerge — not to sprinkle or pour. Every recorded New Testament baptism involves going down into and coming up out of water (Mark 1:10; Acts 8:38-39). And immersion alone preserves the burial-and-resurrection symbolism that Paul identifies as the theological heart of the practice (Romans 6:4; Colossians 2:12). The mode is not arbitrary; it is inseparable from the meaning.\n\n**Subjects: believing individuals.** Because the New Testament consistently links baptism to repentance and faith (Acts 2:38; Acts 8:36-37; Acts 16:31-33), Adventists baptize those who have made a conscious, informed decision to follow Christ. This normally means adolescents and adults, though the church recognizes that children mature at different rates and that readiness involves spiritual understanding, not merely a minimum age. The corrective boundary here is important: Adventists do not reject the sincerity of Christians baptized in other traditions. What we affirm is that Scripture prescribes a specific form and sequence — and that following Christ's own example and command is an act of faithful obedience.\n\n**Significance: covenant, identity, community.** Baptism marks the beginning of covenant life. The candidate publicly declares allegiance to Christ, receives the community's welcome, and enters into the mutual obligations of church fellowship. It is not the end of a process but the beginning of a journey. Adventist churches therefore have a responsibility that extends far beyond the baptismal service: the local congregation commits to mentoring, teaching, and supporting the new member. A baptism without follow-up is an incomplete obedience on the church's part.\n\n**Christ's baptism as model.** Jesus' insistence on being baptized despite having no personal need for repentance establishes the rite's authority and dignity. If the sinless Son of God submitted to baptism, no disciple is above the practice. His baptism also revealed the Trinitarian dimension — Father speaking, Spirit descending, Son emerging from the water — that shapes the Adventist understanding of baptism as an encounter with the full Godhead.\n\n**What this belief guards against.** This formulation guards against treating baptism as either a meaningless tradition (empty ritual without transformed life) or a magical guarantee (the water saves apart from faith). Baptism is the believing response of a repentant heart, enacted in the form Christ Himself prescribed.",
