    id: "w3s-15-3-2",
    lessonId: "w3l-15-3",
    sectionType: "explain",
    title: "What Does This Mean?",
    content: "The story of the Philippian jailer in Acts 16:30-33 illustrates what baptism looks like in practice. The sequence is compressed and vivid: the jailer asks what he must do to be saved, Paul and Silas proclaim the gospel, the jailer responds with faith, and \"immediately he and all his family were baptized\" — at midnight, in a prison, with fresh wounds still being tended. There is no delay, no waiting period, no elaborate ceremony. The urgency reflects the New Testament's understanding of baptism as the natural, immediate expression of a converted heart. The household dimension is also significant: faith rippled outward from the jailer to his family, and the community of faith expanded on the spot.\n\nGalatians 3:27 introduces the metaphor of clothing: \"As many of you as were baptized into Christ have put on Christ.\" In the ancient world, putting on a garment signified taking on a new identity — a soldier's armor, a ruler's robe, a priest's vestments. Paul is saying that baptism clothes the believer in Christ Himself. The old identity — defined by sin, shame, and social category — is covered. What the world now sees, when it looks at the baptized believer, is Christ. This is not a costume to be worn on Sabbath and removed on Monday; it is a permanent new wardrobe that shapes how the believer walks through every hour of every day.\n\nTitus 3:5-7 balances several elements with precision. Salvation is \"not by works of righteousness which we have done\" — merit is excluded. It comes \"through the washing of regeneration and renewing of the Holy Spirit\" — the washing points to baptism, but the regenerating power belongs to the Spirit. And the result is that believers become \"heirs according to the hope of eternal life\" — baptism initiates a life that stretches beyond death into eternity. The passage holds together grace, Spirit, water, and hope in a single breath, refusing to isolate any one element from the others.",
