    lessonId: "w3l-15-3",
    sectionType: "integrate",
    title: "SDA Theological Integration",
    content: "The practical implications of baptism extend far beyond the ceremony itself:\n\n**Baptism as the beginning, not the destination.** A persistent danger in any church is treating baptism as the finish line — the moment of arrival after which commitment can relax. The Adventist understanding insists that baptism is the starting gate of covenant life. The candidate pledges to walk in newness of life (Romans 6:4), and the congregation pledges to nurture, teach, and accompany them. Churches that celebrate baptisms enthusiastically but neglect new-member integration are fulfilling half the commission.\n\n**The local church as baptismal community.** Baptism is not a private transaction between the individual and God — it is a public act performed within and received by a believing community. The local church witnesses the baptism, welcomes the new member, and accepts responsibility for their growth. This is why the app you are using right now supports but does not replace the physical church. Studying doctrine, memorizing Scripture, and praying are all essential — but baptism and the community life it inaugurates require embodied presence with real people in a real place.\n\n**Living as those who have 'put on Christ.'** Galatians 3:27 creates a daily accountability: every morning the baptized believer is called to remember whose identity they wear. Decisions about speech, relationships, work, and leisure are shaped by the question: Does this reflect the Christ I have put on? This is not legalism — it is identity. A person wearing a borrowed garment of incalculable worth will naturally treat it with care.\n\n**Baptism and assurance.** Titus 3:5-7 grounds baptism in God's mercy, not human performance. For believers who struggle with doubt or guilt, their baptism stands as a visible, historical anchor: \"On that day, in that water, God received me.\" The memory of baptism is not nostalgia; it is evidence. When feelings waver, the fact of baptism remains — a concrete marker of God's covenant faithfulness.\n\n**What this belief guards against.** The practical doctrine of baptism guards against privatized faith (believing without belonging), delayed obedience (treating baptism as optional or indefinitely postponable), and institutional neglect (baptizing without discipling).",
